@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

quickstep is an engine for QUIC (RFC 9000) and the TLS 1.3 handshake it
depends on, and also for TLS 1.2 and 1.3 over TCP and DTLS 1.2 and 1.3
over UDP.  It is released under the GNU General Public License,
version 3 or any later version.

For QUIC it provides versions 1 and 2 with compatible version negotiation,
streams and flow control, loss recovery with CUBIC or NewReno congestion
control and pacing, address validation (Retry and tokens), 0-RTT, path
validation and migration, DATAGRAM frames, and an endpoint layer that
serves many connections from one socket.  ECN is supported, for an
application that passes the ECN field of datagrams to and from the library;
the server's preferred address is offered and taken up.

The library is the C member of a family: Gumdrop (Java) and Hopf (Rust)
established the architecture, and this project ports it to C under the
conventions of the GNU project.

The goal is an engine on which an HTTP/3 client and server and a DNS
over QUIC (DoQ) implementation can be written.  It is a library of
state machines and codecs.  It owns no sockets, threads or timers: the
embedding application supplies UDP or TCP input and output, a clock,
and, if it wants concurrency, its own event loop.

quickstep is written in ISO C99 and is built with @option{-std=c99}
@option{-pedantic}.  It builds with no compiler warnings, and the build
system is expected to keep it that way.

Please report bugs, including documentation bugs, to
@email{bug-quickstep@@gnu.org}.

