@node Testing
@section Testing

@command{make check} runs the unit tests: a test per module (RFC vectors
where they exist), and loopback tests that pair engines and connections
over a simulated network with a virtual clock (loss, reordering,
duplication, corruption, NAT rebinding).  They are deterministic and use
no sockets, files, programs or wall clock, so they are fast.

@command{make check-integration} runs the integration suite in
@file{integration/}, which uses real I/O and other programs:
interoperability scripts against
independent implementations: @file{integration/interop.sh} (TLS against OpenSSL
and GnuTLS), @file{integration/interop-dtls.sh} and @file{integration/interop-dtls12.sh}
(DTLS against wolfSSL, OpenSSL and GnuTLS) and @file{integration/interop-quic.sh}
(QUIC against quiche: handshakes, loss, Retry, Version Negotiation, key
update, migration, DATAGRAM, 0-RTT and concurrent clients, both
directions).  @file{integration/quic-interop-runner/} holds an endpoint for the
@uref{https://github.com/quic-interop/quic-interop-runner, QUIC interop
runner}, with the image and the GitHub Actions workflow that run it
against other implementations; the integration suite runs it against
itself over loopback.  Scripts skip themselves (exit 77) when their peers are
missing; set @env{GQ_NO_INTEROP} to skip them, @env{GQ_INTEROP_FILTER} to
run scenarios whose names contain a string, and @env{QUICHE_DIR} to name
the directory holding @command{quiche-client} and @command{quiche-server}.
The library is also run under AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.

ML-DSA signatures, keys and certificates (@file{src/sign.c},
@file{src/mldsa.c}), and handshakes over TLS 1.3 and QUIC with them, are
tested everywhere, since libgcrypt does the signing; the certificates for
the handshakes are put together by the tests (@file{tests/tst-mldsa-cert.h})
under a classical CA, which GnuTLS validates whatever its backend.  What
needs a GnuTLS built with the leancrypto backend, which most are not, is
checking that its keys load and that it and the library agree on each
other's signatures, and chains with an ML-DSA signature in them.  Where
there is none, the tests that need it print a note and skip.  Set
@env{GQ_EXPECT_MLDSA} to a non-empty value other than @samp{0} to say that
the backend is expected, and the same tests fail instead, so that a build
that quietly lost it does not look like a pass.  The CI workflow does this
in its @samp{ML-DSA} job, which builds leancrypto, Nettle and GnuTLS from
pinned sources in the @samp{gnutls-mldsa} stage of
@file{integration/quic-interop-runner/Dockerfile} and runs the unit tests
against them (the @samp{check-mldsa} target; run it locally with
@command{docker build --target check-mldsa -f
integration/quic-interop-runner/Dockerfile .}).

