@node Versions
@section Versions

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Transport @tab Versions
@item QUIC @tab QUIC v1 and v2 (@file{conn.h}), with TLS 1.3 only (required by RFC 9001)
@item TCP  @tab TLS 1.3 (@file{tlsconn.h}) or TLS 1.2 (@file{tls12conn.h})
@item UDP  @tab DTLS 1.3 (@file{dtls.h}) or DTLS 1.2 (@file{dtls12.h})
@end multitable

SSL 3.0, TLS 1.0, TLS 1.1 and DTLS 1.0 are not implemented.

@cindex version negotiation, TLS and DTLS
@cindex downgrade sentinel
Over TCP and UDP the version is negotiated by default
(@file{tlsauto.h}, @file{dtlsauto.h}).  The two engines stay separate
state machines; a thin front decides which one runs.  A server looks at the
ClientHello: if it offers @code{supported_versions} with (D)TLS 1.3 the 1.3
engine serves it, otherwise the 1.2 engine does, so one listener (one port)
serves both kinds of peer and prefers 1.3.  A client sends one ClientHello
that offers both, and continues with whichever the server picked, handing
the transcript to the 1.2 engine if the ServerHello (or, in DTLS, the
HelloVerifyRequest) is a 1.2 one.  The decision is made once, from the
first messages, and is never revisited: neither protocol has
renegotiation into another version, so an association that was 1.2 stays
1.2 and there is nothing to re-run.  A TLS 1.2 server that also serves 1.3
sets the RFC 8446 section 4.1.3 downgrade sentinel in its ServerHello
random, and a negotiating client that offered 1.3 aborts
(@code{illegal_parameter}) when it sees it, so an attacker cannot strip
1.3 from the offer.  Both ends can instead be pinned to one version
(@code{GQ_TLSAUTO_TLS12}, @code{GQ_TLSAUTO_TLS13} and the
@code{GQ_DTLSAUTO_} counterparts), which is what a deployment with a port
per version, or a test, wants; a pinned endpoint refuses the other version
(@code{protocol_version}) and does not set or check the sentinel.  QUIC is
never negotiated: RFC 9001 requires TLS 1.3, so the QUIC engine refuses
anything else and a combined hello is not offered with QUIC.

Some consequences of the combined hello are worth knowing.  The
@code{supported_groups} and @code{signature_algorithms} lists are shared by
both versions, so a 1.2 server may choose secp384r1 for its ECDHE key; the
1.2 client engine accepts that only when its ClientHello also offered 1.3
(a standalone 1.2 client offers secp256r1 and x25519, as @ref{TLS 1.2
hardening} says).  In every case the key exchange group has to be one the hello
listed: a ServerKeyExchange naming another is refused
(@code{illegal_parameter}).  A resuming client's hello, with its ticket
of up to 2 KB, is kept whole for the hand-over however large it is, over
TCP and, in fragments, over DTLS.  Over DTLS a stateless listener must
decide from the first datagram: a ClientHello with a cookie in its
@code{legacy_cookie} field answers a DTLS 1.2 HelloVerifyRequest, one with
the @code{cookie} extension a DTLS 1.3 HelloRetryRequest, and a first
ClientHello by its @code{supported_versions}.  A listener that serves
DTLS 1.3 cannot take a first ClientHello spread over several datagrams,
which would need the state a cookie exchange avoids (the cookie holds the
hash of the whole message); a DTLS 1.2 listener can, as its
HelloVerifyRequest depends only on the first fragment.  Without a listener
a server keeps the first datagrams of a fragmented ClientHello until
@code{supported_versions} has arrived (or eight datagrams have), rather
than guess DTLS 1.2.

@node Cipher suites
@section Cipher suites

AEAD only.

@itemize @bullet
@item
TLS 1.3 and DTLS 1.3: @code{TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384},
@code{TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256}, @code{TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256}.

@item
TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2: ECDHE with ECDSA or RSA authentication and AES-GCM
or ChaCha20-Poly1305 (six suites).
@end itemize

Never implemented: CBC suites of any kind (MAC-then-encrypt has a long
record of timing side channels, Lucky Thirteen and its successors), RC4,
3DES, export and NULL suites, static RSA key transport (no forward
secrecy), finite-field Diffie-Hellman, CCM, anonymous suites, and
PSK-only TLS 1.2.

@node TLS 1.2 hardening
@section TLS 1.2 hardening
@cindex TLS 1.2

TLS 1.2 exists for legacy interoperability (mail, older clients) and is a
deliberately narrower profile than TLS 1.3, the same as in Gumdrop's
@code{Tls12HandshakeEngine} and Hopf's @code{tls12} engine.  The engine
(@file{tls12.h}) is a separate state machine from the TLS 1.3 one, and the
byte-stream wrapper (@file{tls12conn.h}) mirrors @file{tlsconn.h}.  An
association is one or the other; which one is negotiated at the start
(@ref{Versions}) and never changes.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Key exchange is ECDHE on @code{secp256r1} or @code{x25519} (RFC 8422).
There is no static RSA key transport, no finite-field Diffie-Hellman, no
other curve and no hybrid group on this path.  A client offers both in
@code{supported_groups}, P-256 first; a server picks the first group of its
own preference that the client offers, secp256r1 then x25519 unless
the @code{groups} of the configuration say otherwise (the entries that are
one of those two, in the order given; any other group is for TLS 1.3 only
and is ignored, and a list with nothing left means the default).  A client
that sends no @code{supported_groups} is given secp256r1, as RFC 8422
section 5.1.1 allows the server to choose; one that offers none of the
server's groups is refused (@code{handshake_failure}), unless it is
resuming, which needs no key exchange.  A client refuses a group it did not
offer.  The group used is in @code{gq_tls_info.group}, and is 0 for a resumed
session.

@item
Suites are the six ECDHE plus AEAD suites of the policy.  ECDSA
certificates (P-256 and P-384) use the @code{ECDHE_ECDSA} suites and RSA
certificates the @code{ECDHE_RSA} suites; the server only selects a suite
that fits its key.

@item
Signatures: ECDSA, Ed25519 (RFC 8422 section 5.1.3, under the
@code{ECDHE_ECDSA} suites) and RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 with SHA-2 are produced; the
engine never signs with RSA-PSS, but accepts it from a peer.  There is no
SHA-1, no MD5 and no ML-DSA on this path.  A ClientHello without
@code{signature_algorithms} is refused rather than assumed to mean SHA-1.

@item
Alerts: only @code{close_notify} and an alert of fatal level end a
connection (RFC 5246 section 7.2.2); a warning such as
@code{unrecognized_name}, @code{no_renegotiation} or @code{user_canceled}
is ignored.  TLS 1.3 sends the severity in the alert's type: every alert
but @code{close_notify} and @code{user_canceled} is an error whatever its
level field says, and @code{user_canceled} is ignored (RFC 8446 section 6),
over DTLS too.

@item
Extended master secret (RFC 7627) is mandatory.  A peer that does not
offer or echo it is refused, closing the triple-handshake class of
attacks.  The non-extended derivation is not implemented.

@item
Secure renegotiation indication (RFC 5746) is required (the empty
extension, or the signalling suite value from a client), and renegotiation
itself is refused: any handshake message after the handshake, including a
HelloRequest or a ClientHello, is fatal with @code{no_renegotiation}.

@item
No compression (CRIME) and no heartbeat.  A ClientHello offering
anything but the null compression method is refused.

@item
Session resumption by RFC 5077 tickets only, sealed under the same
rotating key ring as TLS 1.3 tickets; there is no session-ID cache.  A
ticket carries the master secret, so there is no forward secrecy across
resumptions: the ticket lifetime (default one day, at most seven) and key
rotation are the mitigation.  A resumed session issues no new ticket, so a
session cannot be extended past its original lifetime.  A ticket is
honoured only if it decrypts, is unexpired, names a suite the client
still offers and the server still allows, was issued for the same server
name, and (when client certificates are required) came from an
authenticated session; otherwise a full handshake runs.  TLS 1.3 tickets
are never accepted by this engine.

@item
Client certificates: the server sends a CertificateRequest and verifies
the chain against its trust store or callback.  The certificate must be
valid for TLS client authentication.  A required client that sends none is
refused with @code{handshake_failure}.

@item
There is no in-protocol rekeying in TLS 1.2, so an AES-GCM direction that
reaches 2^24 records ends the connection (with @code{close_notify}) rather
than exceeding the confidentiality limit.  ChaCha20-Poly1305 is limited
only by the 64-bit sequence number.

@item
No 0-RTT and no KeyUpdate.  The RFC 8446 section 4.1.3 downgrade sentinel
is set by a 1.2 server that also serves 1.3 and checked by a client that
offered 1.3 (@ref{Versions}); a standalone 1.2 endpoint neither sets nor
checks it.  A 1.2 server refuses a ClientHello that lists
@code{supported_versions} without TLS 1.2 (@code{protocol_version}); a
standalone 1.2 client refuses a ServerHello that carries the extension.
@end itemize

@node DTLS 1.3
@section DTLS 1.3
@cindex DTLS
@cindex cookie

DTLS 1.3 (RFC 9147) is the TLS 1.3 handshake engine in DTLS mode, so the
suites, groups, signature schemes, resumption and client authentication
are exactly those of TLS 1.3, including the hybrid and pure post-quantum
groups.
What is DTLS's own is in @file{dtlsrec.h}, @file{dtlshs.h},
@file{dtls.h} and @file{dtlscookie.h}, and follows RFC 9147:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Every HKDF label has the @samp{dtls13} prefix (no trailing space), and
record sequence numbers are encrypted with a per-epoch key, using the
same mask construction as QUIC header protection.  The nonce uses the
64-bit sequence number without the epoch.

@item
Records carry a 16-bit sequence number and an explicit length; records
with a connection ID are not ours (connection IDs are not implemented).
A record that fails authentication or is replayed is dropped silently,
never answered, and a plaintext alert is honoured only while nothing has
been protected yet, so nothing that can be forged from outside kills an
association.

@item
Handshake messages are fragmented to the datagram size and reassembled,
whatever the order, duplication and overlap.  Only the message being
assembled is kept (a later message is dropped and asked for again), so
the memory per association is bounded by one message.

@item
Handshake flights are retransmitted with the RFC 9147 backoff (1 s
doubling to 60 s, six retries by default), fragment by fragment: what
the peer has acknowledged is not sent again.  ACKs are sent for the last
flight of the handshake, for tickets and key updates, and when a flight
arrives out of order or incomplete.

@item
KeyUpdate moves to the next epoch, and the sender switches only when its
KeyUpdate is acknowledged.  Rekeying is automatic before the AEAD record
limit.

@item
Address validation is stateless.  @code{gq_dtls_listen} answers a first
ClientHello with a HelloRetryRequest whose cookie (16 bytes of
HMAC-SHA256 over the client address and the cookie's own fields,
rotating secrets) also carries what the transcript needs, so a source
that cannot receive at its address never gets an association, and the
reply is always smaller than the request.  The client therefore sends a
small first ClientHello (one classical key share, or none when only
post-quantum groups are configured) and puts the cookie first in the second;
the server's retry then asks for its preferred group, so post-quantum
groups are still used.  A hello with no key share at all is shorter than
the retry that would answer it, which the listener never sends, so a
client behind the listener lists a plain-curve group as well as the
post-quantum ones.

@item
Not implemented: 0-RTT (early data), connection IDs and post-handshake
client authentication.  DTLS 1.2 is @ref{DTLS 1.2}.
@end itemize

Verified against wolfSSL 5.9 as an independent DTLS 1.3 peer in both
directions (@file{integration/interop-dtls.sh}), including cookies, hybrid
groups, fragmentation, packet loss, KeyUpdate, resumption and client
certificates.

@node DTLS 1.2
@section DTLS 1.2
@cindex DTLS 1.2

DTLS 1.2 (RFC 6347) is the TLS 1.2 engine in DTLS mode, so it has exactly
the profile of @ref{TLS 1.2 hardening}: ECDHE on @code{secp256r1} or
@code{x25519} with AEAD suites, mandatory extended master secret and secure renegotiation
indication, RFC 5077 tickets, no compression, no renegotiation.  A peer
built without RFC 5746 (wolfSSL without secure renegotiation, for
example) is therefore refused.  What is DTLS's own is in
@file{dtls12rec.h}, @file{dtls12.h} and @file{dtls12cookie.h}:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Records have a cleartext header with a 16-bit epoch and 48-bit sequence
number; epoch 1 begins at the ChangeCipherSpec.  The epoch and sequence
number are part of the additional data and of the ChaCha20-Poly1305
nonce; an AES-GCM record's explicit nonce is taken from the wire, since
the sender chooses it.  Bad records are dropped silently, replays are
caught by a 64-record window per epoch, and there is no rekeying: an
AES-GCM direction stops at 2^24 records.

@item
The transcript holds the 12-byte DTLS handshake header with the
message_seq and no fragmentation (unlike DTLS 1.3, which hashes the TLS
form), and message_seq continues across the cookie exchange.  The engine
keeps the counters; reassembly is the code DTLS 1.3 uses.

@item
Reliability is RFC 6347's, which is simpler than DTLS 1.3's: the last
flight is sent again in full, with fresh record numbers and the same
epochs, on a timer (1 s doubling to 60 s, six retries) and whenever the
peer repeats its own last flight.  There are no ACKs.  A flight stays on
its timer until the engine answers the peer, even after part of the
peer's reply has arrived, because the rest of that reply can still be
lost.  The handshake's final flight has no reply, so it is kept without
a timer and only answers repetitions.  A ChangeCipherSpec that overtakes
the message before it waits until the engine can take it.

@item
Address validation is the HelloVerifyRequest.  @code{gq_dtls12_listen}
answers a first ClientHello with a cookie that is an HMAC over the
address and the parts of the hello that must not change, so nothing is
remembered, and the reply reuses the ClientHello's record number as the
RFC requires.  The client answers a HelloVerifyRequest itself and the
transcript excludes the first hello and the request.
@end itemize

Verified in both directions against OpenSSL 3, GnuTLS 3.8 and wolfSSL
(@file{integration/interop-dtls12.sh}), including cookies, fragmentation and
packet loss, resumption and client certificates.

@node QUIC policy
@section QUIC

QUIC endpoints speak versions 1 and 2 only.  A server accepts an Initial
only in a datagram of at least 1200 bytes, and answers unvalidated clients
at no more than three times what they sent (a client proves its address by
a token or by completing the handshake); Retry is available always or
under load, and Retry and NEW_TOKEN tokens are authenticated, bound to the
client address and expire.  A forged Version Negotiation packet cannot
downgrade a connection (@ref{Version negotiation}).  Session tickets are
bound to the QUIC version, and early data is accepted at most once per
ticket, only with an anti-replay hook, and never without the application
being able to tell it is replayable (@ref{Early data}).  Unknown
destination IDs draw a rate limited stateless reset that is smaller than
its trigger.  Packet protection keys are updated before the AEAD limits,
and a path's amplification limit applies to every unvalidated address
(@ref{Path validation and migration}).

@node Key exchange
@section Key exchange

Hybrid post-quantum groups are preferred (@code{X25519MLKEM768},
@code{SecP256r1MLKEM768}, @code{SecP384r1MLKEM1024}), then X25519,
secp256r1 and secp384r1.  There is no secp521r1, no finite-field group
and no legacy curve.  TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2 use secp256r1 and x25519 only
(@ref{TLS 1.2 hardening}).

The pure ML-KEM groups of draft-ietf-tls-mlkem (@code{MLKEM512},
@code{MLKEM768}, @code{MLKEM1024}) are also supported, in TLS 1.3, DTLS 1.3
and QUIC, for deployments that must not depend on a classical component
(CNSA 2.0, for instance).  They are not in the default list, so the default
stays hybrid first: a connection uses one only when the application puts it
in the @code{groups} of its configuration, and the other end must offer it
too.  A client that lists only pure groups sends one key share, the
ML-KEM encapsulation key, and no classical share; if it lists a plain-curve
group as well, it sends a share for that too, as it does for a hybrid
group, so that a peer without post-quantum support does not force a
HelloRetryRequest.  A server takes the groups in the order it is given, so
an application that lists both hybrid and pure groups decides which it
prefers; @code{gq_policy_supported_groups} returns every group the policy
permits in its own order, which puts the hybrids first, then the pure
groups (768, 1024, 512), then the classical ones.  A key share is the
encapsulation key (800, 1184 or 1568 bytes) from the client and the
ciphertext (768, 1088 or 1568 bytes) from the server, nothing else; a
share of any other length is refused with @code{illegal_parameter}.

@node Signatures and certificates
@section Signatures and certificates

ECDSA (P-256 and P-384), Ed25519, RSA-PSS (RSAE), and ML-DSA-44, 65 and 87
in TLS 1.3 (@ref{Key exchange and signatures}).  There are no RSA PKCS#1
v1.5 signatures in the TLS 1.3 handshake, no SHA-1 and no MD5.

Certificate chains signed with SHA-1 or MD5, or containing RSA keys under
2048 bits, are rejected by the trust layer (GnuTLS verification profile
@samp{MEDIUM}, 112-bit security).  Host names are checked against
subjectAltName only (RFC 9525).

@subsection The size of an ML-DSA handshake
@cindex ML-DSA, handshake size

ML-DSA public keys are 1312, 1952 and 2592 bytes (ML-DSA-44, 65, 87) and
signatures 2420, 3309 and 4627.  A server's Certificate for a leaf and one
intermediate, and its CertificateVerify, come to about 8, 11 and 15 KB, where
a classical flight is under 1 KB.  The tests run handshakes of those sizes
(@file{tests/test-conn.c}, @file{test-dtls.c}, @file{test-tlsconn.c}), with
certificates padded to the sizes so that they do not depend on the ML-DSA of
GnuTLS, and with real ML-DSA keys where GnuTLS can validate them; this is
what they found.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{QUIC}: a server may send only three times what it has received
until the client's address is validated (RFC 9000 section 8.1), and a
client's first flight is one or two Initial datagrams of 1200 bytes, so
3.6 to 7.2 KB of the server's flight can go at once and the rest waits for
the client's reply.  A handshake whose flight is bigger takes @strong{2
round trips} for the client to be connected (2.5 for the server), one more
than the 1 (and 1.5) of a classical one or of a small ML-DSA flight; not
more, up to 21 KB of flight, because once the address is validated the
window (12000 bytes) takes the rest in one go.  The limit is kept: with the
client's packets stopped after the first, the server sends no more than
three times what it received, however long it retransmits.  With loss (up to
15%), reordering and the loss of any one datagram of the start of the flight,
the handshake still completes.  The ClientHello of the post-quantum groups
(1.4 to 2 KB) is more than one Initial datagram, and is read from two
Initial packets whichever is lost.

@item
@strong{DTLS 1.3}: there is no such limit, so the flight of 14 to 25
datagrams goes out at once and the client is connected after 1 round trip
(2 behind the stateless cookie of @file{dtlscookie.h}, as for a classical
flight).  The flight is retransmitted as a whole, and the ACKs of RFC 9147
section 7 and the timer cope with it under loss, reordering and duplication.

@item
@strong{TLS over TCP} and its record layer: the flight spans two or three
records of 16 KiB, which are reassembled whole or fed a byte at a time.  A
handshake message is limited to @code{max_message_len} bytes (65536 by
default), which is a limit on the body of a message, exactly: the
biggest Certificate message of a chain of ML-DSA-87 certificates is 22 KB,
far inside it, and a peer that sends one a byte over is refused with
@code{illegal_parameter}.
@end itemize

@node Server behaviour
@section Server behaviour

@itemize @bullet
@item
The server chooses the cipher suite and group by its own preference
list, but never asks for a retry when the client already sent an
acceptable key share.

@item
The TLS 1.3 server engine (@file{tls.h}, @file{tlsconn.h}, and QUIC)
refuses a ClientHello without @code{supported_versions} offering TLS 1.3,
with @code{protocol_version}; it never falls back to an older protocol
itself.  TLS 1.2 is served by its own engine (@file{tls12.h},
@file{tls12conn.h}), and the negotiating front of @ref{Versions}
(@file{tlsauto.h}, @file{dtlsauto.h}) lets one listener serve both by
picking the engine from the ClientHello; QUIC always uses the 1.3 engine
directly.  The 1.2 engine has the mirror-image rule, in @ref{TLS 1.2
hardening}.

@item
A client certificate must be valid for TLS client authentication (the
@code{clientAuth} purpose), signed by a trusted CA, and must sign the
handshake with a scheme the server listed.  A server never asks for
client certificates unless it was given a way to verify them.

@item
Resumption uses (EC)DHE with the PSK, so every resumed session still gets
forward secrecy; PSK-only resumption is not implemented.  Tickets are
encrypted and authenticated under a key ring the server controls, bound
to the server name, expire after at most seven days, and are refused if
the suite is no longer allowed or, when client certificates are
required, if the original session was not authenticated.  A wrong binder
is a fatal error, not a fallback.
@end itemize

@node Other rules
@section Other rules

@itemize @bullet
@item
0-RTT early data is opt-in per endpoint and needs an anti-replay
strategy; see @ref{Early data}.

@item
AEAD usage limits (RFC 8446 section 5.5, RFC 9001 section 6.6) trigger
rekeying or close.

@item
OCSP stapling is not part of the handshake engines.  Revocation is a
policy question for the embedding application, which receives the peer's
chain.

@item
Key material is wiped after use.  The library never logs secrets.
@end itemize

@node Early data
@chapter Early data (0-RTT)
@cindex 0-RTT
@cindex early data
@cindex replay

Early data lets a client that resumes a session send application data
in its first flight.  It has weaker properties than the rest of the
protocol: the data is not forward secret, and it can be replayed by an
attacker who records the first flight.  quickstep therefore never enables
it by default and never accepts it without an anti-replay check.

@subheading Server

The server accepts early data for a connection only if all of the
following hold:

@itemize @bullet
@item
The endpoint enabled it (@code{max_early_data} is nonzero) and the
embedding layer can deliver it.

@item
The client offered the @code{early_data} extension together with a
resumption PSK, and the server accepted that PSK.

@item
The ticket allows early data and the ALPN protocol selected now is the
one negotiated in the original connection.

@item
The ticket age reported by the client agrees with the server's own
measurement to within the configured window (ten seconds by default).

@item
The application's replay hook reports the ticket as unused.  The library
supplies @code{gq_replay_cache}, a bounded single-use ticket cache that
fails closed when it is full.  If no hook is configured, early data is
declined.

@item
No HelloRetryRequest was needed.
@end itemize

Declining early data does not decline the resumption.  The server
continues with a 1-RTT handshake and skips the client's undecryptable
early records within a budget, without processing them.  Over TCP the
client ends its early data with EndOfEarlyData; in QUIC there is no such
message and the transport carries the early data in 0-RTT packets.

@subheading Client

A client offers early data only when the stored session allows it and
the ALPN protocol it wants matches the one recorded in the session.
After a HelloRetryRequest or a rejection the client discards its early
keys, reports the rejection through the sink so the application can
resend the data as ordinary application data, and continues.

@subheading In QUIC

The QUIC transport carries early data in 0-RTT packets, which belong to
the application data packet number space.

@subsubheading Client
The connection is given the session (@code{gq_tls_config.resume} with
@code{early_data} set) and the server's transport parameters from the
connection that issued it (@code{resume_params}, which the application
keeps alongside the ticket; @code{gq_conn_get_peer_params} produces them).
Until the server's own parameters arrive the remembered ones stand in:
streams can be opened and written and datagrams sent at once, and 0-RTT
packets ride behind the Initial in the first datagrams.  The session must
come from a connection of the version this one starts in, which the
application pins (tickets are version bound, @ref{Version negotiation}).
The @code{early_data_result} event reports the verdict.  If the server
accepts, its parameters must not be lower than the remembered ones or the
connection ends with a protocol violation.  If it rejects, whatever was
sent early is treated as never sent: stream data goes out again from the
start in 1-RTT packets under the real limits, datagrams are reported lost,
and the 0-RTT keys are discarded.

@subsubheading Server
The server engine derives the early read keys from the ticket; 0-RTT
packets that arrive after the ClientHello are decrypted with them and
processed in the application space, with ACK, CRYPTO, NEW_TOKEN,
PATH_RESPONSE and HANDSHAKE_DONE forbidden.  Early packets that arrive
before the ClientHello are dropped and sent again by the client.  The
early keys are discarded three probe timeouts after the handshake
completes.  While the callbacks for early data run,
@code{gq_conn_early_data_active} is true: the data can be replayed, so do
only what is safe to do twice.  A server that switched the connection to
another version ignores 0-RTT.  The endpoint's @code{tickets} and
@code{early_data} options provide the ticket keys and a replay cache.

@node QUIC connections
@chapter QUIC connections

@cindex connection, QUIC
@cindex stream state
A @code{gq_conn} (@file{conn.h}) is one QUIC connection, as a state
machine that owns no socket, thread or timer.  The caller moves datagrams
and time:

@table @code
@item gq_conn_recv
A datagram arrived.  It is decrypted in place.
@item gq_conn_send
Ask for the next datagram to transmit; call until it yields none.
@item gq_conn_timeout
When to call @code{gq_conn_on_timeout} next.
@end table

Time is a caller-supplied monotonic clock in microseconds, so tests run
the same code under a simulated clock.  Application events (connected,
stream opened, stream data, reset, stop sending, closed, connection ID
issued and retired) reach the caller through callbacks that run inside
the call that caused them.

@heading Streams
@file{stream.h} holds the two halves of a stream as separate objects.
The send half buffers only bytes not yet acknowledged, hands out lost
ranges again before new data, and holds new data back by the peer's
credit.  The receive half delivers bytes straight to the callback when
they continue what was delivered before, copies only data that arrives
ahead of a gap, and enforces the final size, flow control and reset rules
of RFC 9000 section 3.  Both use @file{ranges.h}, a bounded sorted set of
half-open ranges, so a peer cannot make their bookkeeping grow without
limit.

@heading Packets that come before their keys
Datagrams can be reordered, so a Handshake or 1-RTT packet can arrive before
the handshake data that gives the keys to read it (the server's flight
arriving with its Initial last, the client's 1-RTT packet ahead of its
Finished), and a 0-RTT packet before the ClientHello.  RFC 9001 section 5.7
lets an endpoint drop them, which costs the peer a retransmission.  Instead
a connection holds up to @code{gq_conn_config.max_buffered_packets} (16 by
default; negative for none) of them, if their connection IDs, and for a
Handshake packet the version, are ones the connection could have, and reads
them, in the order they came and as of the time they came, when the keys
arrive.  Past the limit a packet is dropped (qlog @code{rejected}); what is
still unreadable when its keys are discarded, or the connection ends, is
freed.  A held packet counts towards the amplification limit when it arrives,
and not again.  Each can take a datagram's worth of memory.  An endpoint
holds, up to eight datagrams for a second, 0-RTT packets for a connection that
does not exist yet, and gives them to it when the client's Initial makes it.
qlog has @code{packet_buffered} (trigger @code{keys_unavailable}) when a
packet is held and @code{keys_available} on its @code{packet_received}
later; @code{gq_conn_stats} counts @code{packets_buffered}.

@heading What a connection does
The TLS 1.3 engine runs in QUIC mode; its output is queued as CRYPTO data
per packet number space and its secrets become packet keys.  Received
packets are protected and deprotected in place; coalesced packets are
handled; duplicates are dropped by packet number.  ACKs are sent at once
for the handshake spaces and after two packets or 25 ms otherwise (at
once on a gap).  Loss detection follows RFC 9002 (packet and time
thresholds, probe timeouts with back-off) and a congestion controller
limits what is in flight (@ref{Congestion control}); what a lost packet carried is
put back on the queues frame by frame.  A server obeys the
anti-amplification limit and pads datagrams that carry ack-eliciting
Initial packets to 1200 bytes; a client pads all its Initial datagrams.
Keys are discarded as the handshake progresses, and the 1-RTT keys are
updated on request, when the peer updates, and before the AEAD limit.

@heading DATAGRAM
Unreliable datagrams (RFC 9221) are enabled with
@code{max_datagram_frame_size} in the configuration (0, the default,
leaves them off) and need the peer to enable them too.
@code{gq_conn_datagram_send} queues one (a bounded queue) and returns an
id; it goes out ahead of stream data in the next packet with room, under
congestion control, and is never retransmitted.  The @code{datagram}
event delivers received ones, and @code{datagram_acked} and
@code{datagram_lost} report the fate of each sent one by id.

@heading Limits of this step
The receiving side treats a few limits as fixed: 64 tracked packet number ranges per space and 8 connection IDs
in each direction.

@node Congestion control
@chapter Congestion control

@cindex congestion control
@cindex pacing
@cindex CUBIC
@cindex NewReno
@cindex HyStart++
What a connection may have in flight is limited by a congestion window
(@file{src/cc.c}, apart from the connection and testable on its own).
@code{gq_conn_config.congestion_control} chooses the controller:
@code{GQ_CC_CUBIC} (the default; RFC 9438) or @code{GQ_CC_NEWRENO} (RFC 9002
section 7).  Both start from the initial window of RFC 9002 section 7.2
(ten datagrams) and share slow start, recovery periods and persistent
congestion; they differ in how far the window falls on loss (0.7 for CUBIC,
0.5 for NewReno) and how it grows afterwards.  CUBIC follows
@math{C (t - K)^3 + W_max} from the reduced window through a plateau at the
window where the loss happened and on beyond it, grows like Reno while that
would be faster (a small window on a short path), and lowers @math{W_max}
further when a loss comes before the last maximum was regained (fast
convergence).

@heading Slow start
Slow start runs HyStart++ (RFC 9406) for both controllers, so that a path
with a deep queue is not overrun every time.  Each round trip's minimum
round trip time is compared with the previous round's; a rise of an eighth
(bounded to between 4 and 16 ms), seen in at least eight samples, slows the
growth to a quarter, and five such rounds end slow start without waiting
for a loss.  A round trip time that falls back returns to normal slow start.

@heading Pacing
Bulk stream data is paced (RFC 9002 section 7.7): a token bucket spreads the
window over the round trip at 1.25 windows per round trip, or 2 in slow
start, rather than putting it on the wire back to back.  From idle the
initial window may leave at once.  With data in flight, credit is held to one
datagram plus a millisecond's worth at the current rate: enough for any rate
with a millisecond timer, while at the rates a contended bottleneck allows it
releases datagrams one at a time instead of the pairs an acknowledgement
would otherwise free together, which lose a packet at a nearly full queue
where a single one gets through.  Nothing is paced until the first round
trip sample exists.  Acknowledgements, probes, handshake data, path
validation, DATAGRAM frames and close are not paced.

When the pacer holds stream data back, @code{gq_conn_timeout} returns the
time it will have credit, so a caller that sends when its timer fires needs
nothing more.  Credit accrues from the time of the check, not the time it
was due: a late timer makes the next release larger and never lowers the
average rate.

@heading When the window grows
The window grows only while it is in use (RFC 9002 section 7.8): when the
last attempt to send ended because the window or the pacer would not let
more out, not for lack of data or because of the peer's flow control.  On a
change of path the window, recovery state and pacer start again.
@code{gq_conn_get_stats} reports @code{cwnd}, @code{ssthresh},
@code{congestion_events} and @code{pacing_rate_bps}.

@heading ECN
The library does not touch sockets, so ECN (RFC 9000 section 13.4, RFC 9002
section 7.1) needs the application: it gives each datagram's ECN field (a
@code{gq_ecn}, from @code{IP_TOS} or the ancillary data of the socket) to
@code{gq_conn_recv_ecn}, or @code{gq_endpoint_recv_ecn}, and sets the field
that @code{gq_conn_send_ecn}, or @code{gq_endpoint_send_ecn}, returns on the
datagram it sends.  The plain calls do neither, and a connection that
only has them never marks anything.

Received packets are counted by codepoint in each packet number space and
the ACK frames say so (type 0x03); a packet marked CE is acknowledged at
once.  For sending, only 1-RTT packets on the current path are marked
(ECT(0)), never the handshake, so a path that drops the marks cannot hurt it.
The first ten are the test: after that none are marked until an ACK frame
shows the peer received a marked packet and counted it, with counts that
add up.  It fails, and marking ends, if the counts are missing, fall, add
up to less than the marked packets newly acknowledged, include ECT(1), or
come to more than were sent, or if all of the test packets are lost.  A new
path starts the test again.  Once it has passed, an increase in the CE
count is a congestion event like a loss, with the window reduced once for
the round trip (never persistent congestion).  @code{gq_conn_get_stats}
reports @code{ecn_state}, @code{ecn_marked_sent} and the counts received,
and qlog has @code{ecn_state_updated} and the codepoint of each datagram.

@heading Path MTU discovery
Datagrams start at 1200 bytes, which every path carries (RFC 9000 section
14), and grow (RFC 9000 section 14.3, RFC 8899).  Once the handshake is
confirmed a probe is sent: a PING and padding in a datagram of the size to
try, alone, so that it is acknowledged if the path takes the size and lost if
not.  @code{gq_conn_config.max_udp_payload} is the ceiling, 1452 by default
(within 1200 and 2048), and the peer's @code{max_udp_payload_size} bounds it
too.  The first probe is at the ceiling, which most paths take.  After three
lost probes of a size it is given up, the search halves what is left, and it
ends when the sizes still in question are 16 bytes apart.  Probes are sent
only when the congestion window and the buffer given to @code{gq_conn_send}
(of at least @code{max_udp_payload} bytes) hold them, and not while the
amplification limit applies.

A probe that is lost is not congestion (RFC 9000 section 14.4): it is not
counted as a lost packet, causes no congestion event, does not count towards
persistent congestion and is not marked for ECN.  The size is the path's own.
On a change of path it starts from 1200 and the search is made again; a path
the connection returns to has the size it had.  Three timeouts in a row while
datagrams are larger than 1200 bytes (a path that no longer takes the size,
a ``black hole'') take the size back to 1200 and the search runs below the
old size; and a search that ended below the ceiling is made again after 600
seconds, as the path may have improved.  The congestion controller and the
pacer count in the size in use, so the minimum window and the growth per
round trip follow it.  @code{gq_conn_get_stats} reports
@code{max_datagram_size} and @code{pmtu_done}, and qlog has
@code{mtu_updated}, @code{is_mtu_probe_packet} and the recovery parameters
again at each new size.

An endpoint that only sends small packets, such as one downloading, does not
notice that its size has become too large until it sends something large.
Receive buffers must hold datagrams of @code{max_udp_payload}.

@node qlog
@chapter qlog

@cindex qlog
@cindex logging
@cindex events
A connection can report what it does as the events of
draft-ietf-quic-qlog-quic-events: packets sent, received and dropped,
frames, streams, keys, the recovery metrics, losses, timers, paths.  Like
everything in quickstep it is a callback and nothing else: the library opens
no file, reads no environment variable and writes nothing to stderr.  Set
@code{gq_conn_config.qlog} (@file{quickstep/qlog.h}) and each event arrives
as the connection it belongs to, a time, a name and a line of fields.  With
no callback set, logging costs one pointer test where an event would be.

The draft defines the events, their fields and their units, and leaves the
serialization open.  This one is lines of text, for the standard Unix tools,
and not JSON, so that nothing in the library writes or reads it.  An event
is a time (microseconds, on the clock given to the connection), a name and
the draft's fields flattened to @code{key=value}:

@example
1234567 quic:packet_sent header.packet_type=1RTT header.packet_number=42 \
  frames.0.frame_type=ack frames.0.acked_ranges=3-7,9 \
  frames.1.frame_type=stream frames.1.stream_id=0 frames.1.raw.length=1200
@end example

@noindent
(on one line).  A member of an object is joined to its object by a dot, the
items of a list of objects are numbered, a list of numbers is one
comma-separated value, ACK ranges are written @code{low-high}, durations are
in milliseconds with three decimals, and a space, @samp{%}, control character
or non-ASCII byte in a value is written @code{%XX}, so that a line splits
into fields on spaces and each into key and value on its first @samp{=}.  The
header file has the whole of it.  Finding every lost packet of a trace is

@example
awk '$2 == "quic:packet_lost"' abcd1234_server.qlog.txt
@end example

@heading Detail
@code{qlog_importance} chooses by the draft's levels (Core, Base, Extra; 0,
the default, is all of them), and @code{qlog_raw} how many bytes of the data
of CRYPTO, STREAM, DATAGRAM and NEW_TOKEN frames to include as hex; the
default, none, logs lengths only, since the data can be secret or personal.
Keys are never logged: @code{key_updated} names the key and its phase.  A
connection that logs holds about 48 KiB of buffers for it.

@heading Many connections
An endpoint serves all its connections with one callback, so the callback is
given the connection (a pointer that is the same for every event of it) and
tells them apart by it.  It may ask only what reads the connection, such as
@code{gq_conn_original_dcid}, which names a log by the draft's convention.
The first event of a connection is its @code{connection_started}.  Events
that belong to no connection arrive with a null connection: the server
listening, datagrams that matched no connection, and the Version
Negotiation, Retry and stateless reset packets the endpoint sends itself.
Event times never go back, whatever the caller's clock does between calls.

@code{qlog_addr} tells the library how to write the addresses of a path as
text, which it cannot know; without it the events carry none, as the draft
allows.

@heading What is logged
Every event of the draft except @code{frames_created},
@code{frames_parsed} and @code{frames_processed}, which would repeat what @code{packet_sent} and
@code{packet_received} already carry, which the draft advises against.  A
lost packet lists the frames it carried as far as they were kept, and the
simple stream states (@code{open}, @code{closed}) are used unless the
importance asks for the most, which gets the detailed ones.  Congestion
states are slow start, conservative slow start (HyStart++), congestion
avoidance and recovery.

@heading The interop endpoint
The endpoint of the interop runner (@file{integration/quic-interop-runner})
writes a file for each connection into @env{QLOGDIR}, which the runner sets
and keeps, named by the original destination connection ID and the side
(@file{abcd1234_server.qlog.txt}), and one for the events of no connection.
@command{qlog-lint} checks such files against the draft's event
definitions: the names, the fields each event must and may have, the values
of the enumerated ones, the types of the numbers.  The unit tests run that
check on every event of every scenario of the simulated-network tests, and
the interop workflow on what each run against another implementation wrote.

@node Endpoint
@chapter Endpoint

@cindex endpoint
@file{endpoint.h} puts many connections behind one socket.  Like the
connection it owns no socket, thread or timer: feed it every datagram
(@code{gq_endpoint_recv}), take datagrams to send until it yields none
(@code{gq_endpoint_send}), and call @code{gq_endpoint_on_timeout} when
@code{gq_endpoint_timeout} says.  A serving endpoint is given a TLS server
configuration and an @code{accept} callback that fills in the event
callbacks of each new connection; a client-only endpoint just uses
@code{gq_endpoint_connect}.  Connections it creates belong to it and are
freed after the @code{done} callback.

@heading Routing
Each connection reports the connection IDs it issues and retires (the
router hooks of @file{conn.h}); the endpoint keeps them in a hash table
seeded per endpoint and routes a datagram by its destination ID.  The
client's first destination ID stays routed to the server connection it
created.  All connections of an endpoint use the same ID length.

@cindex QUIC-LB
@cindex connection ID, routable
By default every connection ID a server mints is random, which is fine
for one process but tells an external load balancer nothing.
@file{quiclb.h} implements QUIC-LB (draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers-21): a
connection ID whose first octet carries a config id and, plaintext or
under AES-128 (a single pass at 16 octets, a four-round Feistel
construction otherwise, both from section 5.4), a server identifier the
load balancer decodes without keeping any state.  A
@code{gq_quiclb_config} plugs into @code{gq_conn_config.cid_gen} (every
connection ID a connection mints, including each
@code{NEW_CONNECTION_ID}) and @code{gq_admit_config.cid_gen} (the ID
chosen in a Retry, @ref{Address validation}, which must be routable too
since the client addresses the rest of the handshake to it) through
@code{gq_quiclb_cid_gen}; @code{gq_quiclb_cid_len} gives the length to set
@code{cid_len}/@code{retry_cid_len} to.  A load balancer, or another
server sharing the configuration, recovers the server id with
@code{gq_quiclb_decode_server_id} or checks it with @code{gq_quiclb_is_own}.
Verified against the draft's appendix B test vectors (unencrypted,
single-pass and both four-pass Feistel cases) and, at the connection
level, that every ID a server behind a configuration answers to (the
handshake ID, the spares offered up front, the Retry ID, and the fresh
one an active migration mints after retiring the old one) decodes to its
own server id.

@heading Admission
A long header datagram for an unknown ID goes through
@code{gq_quic_admit} (@ref{Address validation}): Version Negotiation,
Retry (always, or once @code{retry_above} connections exist) or a new
connection, subject to @code{max_connections}.

@heading Stateless reset
A short header datagram for an unknown ID is first checked against the
stateless reset tokens the endpoint's peers gave (a hash table again): a
match means the peer reset a connection and it ends.  Otherwise the
endpoint answers with a stateless reset, at most
@code{max_resets_per_second} of them, smaller than the trigger.  Tokens
derive from a key (HMAC of the connection ID), so an endpoint that lost
its state, or another endpoint holding the same key, answers correctly.

@heading Scheduling
The endpoint remembers which connections may have something to send (the
connection says so after any call that can produce output) and serves them
in turn, so that one busy connection cannot starve the rest.  Deadlines
sit in a heap with lazy invalidation, so the cost of a quiet connection is
nothing until its timer is due.

@node Address validation
@chapter Address validation

@cindex Retry
@cindex token, address validation
A server that answers an unvalidated client may send at most three times
what it received.  A client proves its address by returning a token.
@file{token.h} makes and checks tokens: AES-256-GCM under a rotating
key, with the client's address as associated data, an issue time, and for
Retry the client's original destination ID and the ID the server chose.
Nothing is stored server side.

@file{listen.h} is the stateless step before a connection exists.
@code{gq_quic_admit} looks at a datagram for an unknown connection ID and
decides: drop it, reply (Version Negotiation for an unknown version, or a
Retry when @code{require_retry} is set and no valid token came with it),
or accept, filling a @code{gq_conn_accept} for
@code{gq_conn_server_accept}.  A valid token of either kind marks the
client validated.  A connection created after a Retry uses the ID the
Retry chose and reports the original in its transport parameters.  Once
the handshake completes, a connection created with token keys sends
NEW_TOKEN for the client to use next time (the client receives it through
the @code{new_token} event and passes it as @code{token} in its
configuration).

The client follows a Retry automatically: it verifies the integrity tag,
keeps the token, switches to the new destination ID and Initial keys and
sends its first flight again.  It accepts one Retry, and only before
hearing anything else from the server.

@node Version negotiation
@chapter Version negotiation

@cindex version negotiation
Two mechanisms, both automatic once @code{gq_conn_config.versions} lists
the versions an endpoint is willing to use (most preferred first).

@heading Incompatible: Version Negotiation packets
A server endpoint answers an Initial in a version it does not offer with
a Version Negotiation packet (@code{gq_quic_admit} does this, listing its
own versions).  A client that receives one which echoes its connection
IDs and does not list the version it used starts over with the first of
its versions the server lists: new destination ID, Initial keys and
handshake.  If there is none the connection ends with
@code{GQ_QERR_VERSION_NEGOTIATION} and the closed event.

@heading QUIC version 2
Version 2 (RFC 9369) differs from version 1 in four places only, all in
@file{packet.c} and @file{protect.c}: the version number, the Initial
salt, the HKDF label prefix (@samp{quicv2}) for packet, header protection
and key update keys, the long header packet type bits, and the Retry
integrity key and nonce.  Everything else is shared, so a connection
carries its version and consults it at those points.  Defaults follow
Gumdrop and Hopf: with nothing configured an endpoint lists v2 then v1.

@heading Compatible: RFC 9368
Every connection sends the @code{version_information} transport
parameter: the version in use and the versions available.  QUIC v1 and v2
are compatible, so a server that prefers another version than the one the
client started with (once it has read the client's parameters) answers in
that version, keeping the handshake and the connection.  The client
switches on the first Initial it can decrypt under the new version's
keys; the server keeps accepting the client's Initial packets in the old
version until the client has switched.

A client starts in the oldest version it lists (the one a server is most
likely to parse, RFC 9368 section 2.5) and offers the compatible others
in its preference order; the server takes the client's most preferred
version that it also lists.  Two endpoints with default configuration
therefore start in v1 and finish in v2.

Session tickets are bound to the version (RFC 9369 section 5).  A server
serves version 1 with the ticket key ring it was given and derives a
separate ring for version 2, so a ticket issued under one version fails
to open under the other and the handshake falls back to a full one.  The
server engine reads the client's transport parameters before it reads
tickets, so a compatible switch selects the right ring in time.  A
client should offer a stored session only on a connection that starts in
the version that issued it; the ticket event reports that version.

Downgrade protection: a client checks that the server's chosen version is
the one in use, that a compatible switch came with the parameter, and,
after a Version Negotiation packet, that the version it restarted with is
the one it would have chosen from the server's own list.  A forged
Version Negotiation packet hiding a preferred version is thus caught and
the connection ends with VERSION_NEGOTIATION_ERROR (0x11).  A server
checks that the client's chosen version is the version of its first
Initial.

Only QUIC v1 and v2 are implemented, so compatible negotiation exists
only between them; because they are compatible, a forged Version
Negotiation packet that hides v2 is undone by the server moving the
restarted connection back to the client's preferred version.  Version 2
has been tested against itself and against the RFC 9369 vectors; none of
the peers available for interop (quiche, OpenSSL) speaks it.

@node Path validation and migration
@chapter Path validation and migration

@cindex migration
@cindex path validation
The library never sees a socket, so a network path is a pair of opaque
byte strings the caller chooses (@code{gq_path}: a local and a remote
address, normally packed IP address and port).  Only equality matters.
@code{gq_conn_recv_path} says where a datagram came from;
@code{gq_conn_send_path} says where the datagram it produced must go and
from which local address.  Most datagrams go to the current path;
probing datagrams go elsewhere and must be sent exactly there.  The plain
@code{gq_conn_recv} and @code{gq_conn_send} give no path information and
turn migration handling off.

@heading Validation
A path is validated by a PATH_CHALLENGE and the matching PATH_RESPONSE
(section 8.2): up to three challenges with fresh data over a timeout of at
least three probe timeouts, in datagrams padded to 1200 bytes when the
amplification limit allows.  The peer's challenges are answered on the
path they arrived on.

@heading Peer migration
A packet that carries a non-probing frame, has the highest packet number
so far and comes from a new address means the peer moved (section 9.3).
The connection then sends there at once, using a fresh peer connection ID
when one is available, limited to three times what has arrived from that
address until it validates, and validates it.  The previous path is kept
until then.  If validation fails, the connection goes back to it and
ignores the failed address for ten seconds so a spoofed or unreachable
address cannot make it flap.  On a switch the congestion controller and
RTT estimator start again (section 9.4).  At most three candidate paths
are tracked.

@heading Active migration
@code{gq_conn_probe_path} validates a path without moving to it;
@code{gq_conn_migrate} (clients only, and not if the server set
disable_active_migration) validates and then moves, retiring the old
connection ID.  A client keeps accepting datagrams from the server's
address on any of its sockets, because a server goes on using the old path
until it has validated the new one; datagrams from other addresses are
ignored.  Events report path_validated, path_failed and migrated.

@heading The preferred address
A server can offer clients another address of its own to move to (RFC 9000
section 9.6, for instance to hand a connection to a machine closer to it).
It sets @code{gq_conn_config.preferred_address}, a function called once for
each connection as it starts, with the path the client's first Initial came
by; the function fills in the server's IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and ports, as
it may choose for that client, and returns nonzero to offer them.  The
connection adds a connection ID, of sequence number 1 and not repeated in a
NEW_CONNECTION_ID frame, and its stateless reset token (which an endpoint
derives from its key like any other, so that a restarted server can answer
for it).  The server must receive on, and send from, the addresses it names;
the local address of the path it is given tells it which.

A client keeps the connection ID and token, and when the handshake is
confirmed is told by the @code{preferred_address} event.  It decides
whether and how to go: the library cannot turn the address in the
parameter into the application's own kind of address, so the application
builds the @code{gq_path} (the server's address of a family the socket can
reach, and the local address to send from) and calls
@code{gq_conn_migrate_preferred}.  The path is validated and then used as for
@code{gq_conn_migrate}, with the connection ID that came with the address;
the call is allowed when the server set @code{disable_active_migration}, as
RFC 9000 section 18.2 says.  If validation fails the connection stays on
the original address.

@heading The spin bit
The latency spin bit (RFC 9000 section 17.4) lets an observer on the path
measure the round trip time: a server sends the bit of the newest 1-RTT
packet it has received from the client, and a client the opposite.  A
connection does not use it one time in eight, which is more than the one in
sixteen the RFC requires, and again when it moves to another path, which
starts it from the beginning; it then sends a random bit in each packet
and ignores the one it receives.  Set @code{disable_spin_bit} in
@code{gq_conn_config} to do that for every connection.

@heading Not done
The server's preferred_address, multipath, and choosing among several
local addresses are left to the application.

@node Roadmap
@chapter Roadmap

Each step is testable against RFC vectors or a real peer (OpenSSL, the
GnuTLS command line tools and quiche are available on the development
machine) before the next one starts, as in Hopf's migration plan.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Done.}  Status codes, variable-length integers, frame codec,
crypto facade, trust store and policy.

@item
@strong{Done.}  The packet layer for QUIC versions 1 and 2: long and short
header codecs, packet number encoding, key derivation and key update,
in-place packet protection, the Retry integrity tag, Version
Negotiation, stateless reset and transport parameters.  Verified against
the RFC 9001 appendix A and RFC 9369 appendix A vectors and fuzzed under
ASan and UBSan.  Address validation tokens and RFC 9368 version
information came later (@ref{Address validation}, @ref{Version
negotiation}).

@item
@strong{Done.}  The key exchange facade (X25519, P-256, P-384 and the
three hybrid groups) and the signature facade (ECDSA P-256 and P-384,
Ed25519, RSA-PSS and ML-DSA).

@item
TLS 1.3 handshake engines, QUIC first:

@enumerate a
@item
@strong{Done.}  Message codecs, transcript hash and the key schedule for
SHA-256 and SHA-384.  The RFC 8448 section 3 trace is reproduced end to
end.

@item
@strong{Done.}  The client engine, a reactive state machine with a sink
of callbacks.  It covers HelloRetryRequest (including cookies), SNI,
ALPN, QUIC transport parameters, certificate validation, client
authentication, session tickets, KeyUpdate and the RFC 5705 exporter.  It
sends key shares for a hybrid group plus a plain curve so that servers
without post-quantum support need no retry.

@item
@strong{Done.}  The TLS record layer and TLS over TCP: AEAD records with
inner content types and padding, the compatibility ChangeCipherSpec,
alerts, close_notify and rekeying by record count.  All seven encrypted
records of the RFC 8448 trace are reproduced byte for byte.

@item
@strong{Done.}  Interoperability: the client against @command{openssl
s_server} and @command{gnutls-serv}.

@item
@strong{Done.}  The server engine: cipher suite and group selection by
server preference, HelloRetryRequest with an optional cookie, credential
selection by SNI, signature scheme selection, ALPN, optional or required
client authentication, and refusal of anything below TLS 1.3.  Verified
by a loopback test pairing the client and server engines (including
mutated ClientHellos) and by the interop server driven by
@command{openssl s_client} and @command{gnutls-cli}.

@item
@strong{Done.}  PSK resumption (@code{psk_dhe_ke}) on both sides:
stateless tickets under an AES-256-GCM key ring, binders over the
truncated ClientHello (also after a HelloRetryRequest), obfuscated ticket
age and fallback to a full handshake.  Verified against RFC 8448 section
4 and against real peers.

@item
@strong{Done.}  0-RTT early data with anti-replay: the engines, the
record layer skip budget, the TCP glue and the replay cache
(@ref{Early data}), verified against the RFC 8448 section 4 early
traffic secret; the QUIC transport side is a step of its own below.
@end enumerate

@item
QUIC transport:

@enumerate a
@item
@strong{Done.}  Connection and stream state (@ref{QUIC connections}):
range sets, the stream send and receive halves, and the connection with
its packet number spaces, handshake, ACKs, RTT estimation, loss
detection and probe timeouts (RFC 9002 sections 5 and 6), flow control,
connection IDs, key update, idle timeout, close and stateless reset.
Verified by simulated-network tests (loss, reordering, corruption, tight
flow control) and against quiche in both directions.

@item
@strong{Done.}  Congestion control and pacing (@ref{Congestion control}):
CUBIC (RFC 9438) or NewReno (RFC 9002 section 7), HyStart++ (RFC 9406) in
slow start, persistent congestion, window growth only while the window is
in use, and a token bucket that spreads the window over the round trip
(RFC 9002 section 7.7), and ECN.  Still open: further controllers (BBR).

@item
@strong{Done.}  Address validation (@ref{Address validation}): Retry and
NEW_TOKEN tokens, the stateless admission step, and the client's handling
of Retry.  

@item
@strong{Done.}  Version negotiation (@ref{Version negotiation}): the
client's handling of Version Negotiation packets, RFC 9368 version
information and compatible negotiation between QUIC v1 and v2.

@item
@strong{Done.}  Path validation and migration (@ref{Path validation and
migration}).

@item
@strong{Done.}  DATAGRAM (RFC 9221) and the endpoint layer (@ref{Endpoint}).

@item
@strong{Done.}  QUIC-LB connection IDs (@ref{Endpoint}): a pluggable
connection ID generator that encodes a routable server identifier,
plaintext or AES-encrypted, into every ID a server mints, including in a
Retry.

@item
@strong{Done.}  0-RTT in the QUIC transport (@ref{Early data}).
@end enumerate

@item
@strong{Done.}  The TLS 1.2 engines and record layer over TCP (the
profile of @ref{TLS 1.2 hardening}): key schedule with the PRF
checked against published vectors, the 1.2 record layer (AES-GCM and
ChaCha20-Poly1305), client and server engines with mutual authentication
and RFC 5077 resumption, and the @file{tls12conn.h} wrapper.  Verified by
loopback tests that edit each side's flight to check every refusal, and
by interoperability scenarios against @command{openssl} and GnuTLS in
both directions.

@item
@strong{Done.}  DTLS 1.3 (RFC 9147): the
record layer with record number encryption and anti-replay, handshake
fragmentation and reassembly, flights with ACKs and retransmission,
KeyUpdate, and stateless cookies (@ref{DTLS 1.3}).  Still open for DTLS
1.3: 0-RTT and connection IDs.

@item
@strong{Done.}  DTLS 1.2 (RFC 6347) on the TLS 1.2 engine, with the
DTLS 1.3 fragment reassembly, whole-flight retransmission and a
stateless HelloVerifyRequest listener (@ref{DTLS 1.2}).

@item
@strong{Done.}  Version negotiation for TLS and DTLS (@ref{Versions}):
one listener serves 1.3 and 1.2 peers and prefers 1.3, a client offers
both and falls back, the downgrade sentinel protects the choice, and
either end can be pinned to one version.  Verified in simulation (every
pairing, loss, front door on and off) and against OpenSSL, GnuTLS and
wolfSSL.

@item
Examples: an HTTP/3 (QPACK) client and server and a DoQ (RFC 9250)
client and server, in a separate repository or under @file{examples/}.
@end enumerate

Open decisions: whether to vendor gnulib (probably yes once portability
shims are needed); Encrypted Client Hello and certificate compression,
which Gumdrop has and are deferred here.  (The timer interface was
decided: a ``next timeout'' query, for connections and endpoints alike.)

Not implemented anywhere yet: the server's
preferred_address, multipath, and DTLS 1.3 connection IDs.

@subheading Planned
@itemize @bullet
@item
QUIC-LB configuration rotation (@ref{Endpoint}): when a server adopts a
@code{gq_quiclb_config} with a different config id, re-issue enough
connection IDs under the new one and set @code{retire_prior_to} so the
old ones are retired, as Gumdrop's @code{ConnectionIdManager.rotateTo}
does; a keyed configuration replaces as many IDs as are active, up to
the peer's limit, while an unkeyed one issues a single replacement since
plaintext IDs are not rotated for unlinkability.  Today a
@code{gq_quiclb_config} is fixed for a connection's lifetime.

@item
Gaps found by comparing with Gumdrop and Hopf: certificate compression
(RFC 8879); @code{record_size_limit} (RFC 8449); Encrypted Client Hello
(RFC 9849) including rotation and DTLS; QUIC keep-alive PING; a streams-available event when
MAX_STREAMS raises the limit; segmentation
offload; the server's preferred_address; caching AEAD
handles for speed; asynchronous crypto offload; and a DANE/TLSA verifier.
Whether to send Retry by default is also open.
@end itemize

@node Internationalization
@chapter Internationalization
@cindex gettext
@cindex translation

Every string the library can show a person is translatable with GNU
gettext.  Currently this is the text returned by @code{gq_strerror}.
Protocol names, alert names and other identifiers defined by RFCs are not
translated.

The translation domain is @samp{quickstep}.  The library binds it to the
installed locale directory in @code{gq_crypto_init}, so an application
that calls @code{gq_crypto_init} before anything else needs no other
setup.  The application remains responsible for calling
@code{setlocale}.  The returned strings are owned by the library and
valid until the next locale change.

Translators work in the @file{po/} directory; @file{po/POTFILES.in}
lists the source files that contain marked strings, and each new
user-visible string must be added there.  Strings are marked with the
@code{_} macro from @file{src/gettext.h}.  When the library is
configured with @option{--disable-nls} the macro is the identity.

